Class and Class Members t is a user-defined data-type which contains data members carrying values that
describe the characteristics of the entity the class represents,
When a class is instantiated, an object is created which is provided a memory/storage location.

Data Members are the identifiers that describe the characteristics and hold important data related to the class.
 Anything that can hold data value can be a data-member.
Member Functions/Methods are class-functions/methods which describe the behavior of the class. 
i.e. what kind of operation we can perform with objects of this class.
n C++ can be defined inside/outside the class, however, it is mandatory to have prototype declaration
inside the class if go for an outside-the-class definition.



